{
  "schema": "loonext.rig-scenarios.v2",
  "notes": [
    "The catalogue of what the rig can run, on demand. name and far_end are docs/RIG.md 4.2's own text, and scripts/check-rig-scenarios.mjs fails when they differ, when 4.2's dials per run differ from dials + far_end_legs, or when min_room differs from what 4.1's room table implies. Nothing here says when a scenario runs: the rig runs nothing on a timer.",
    "dials: legs Loonext creates. far_end_legs: legs the far end creates, on the rig the robot's. A run's rig dials are their sum, as in apps/api/e2e/call-journey-budget.json. peak_concurrent_outbound_legs counts Loonext's legs only; the robot holds its own.",
    "Scenarios that name a journey carry the journey's measured values. Every other split between dials and far_end_legs, and every other peak, is a planning estimate that keeps 4.2's dials for the run.",
    "runs: the ways to run a scenario. One run, or one per named variant (S25-2 is the second); the labels are the ones 4.2's row names. A run with dials is admitted by the guard's own caps, window and room, not by anything here.",
    "A run with more dials than the hourly cap cannot be admitted: the guard counts a clock hour, so a run of 4 dials never fits under 3 an hour. check-rig-scenarios.mjs lists them. They stay in the catalogue, and the cap is the guard's own."
  ],
  "caps": {
    "daily_cap": 18,
    "hourly_cap": 3,
    "window_utc": [4, 10],
    "source": "docs/RIG.md 4.1: the guard's own cap, inside the Paid level's 10 an hour and 100 a day account-wide; the window is 3.5's 04:00-10:00 UTC until production has 28 days of call history. The check reads each of these from the doc and fails when one here differs."
  },
